{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>You are children to HaShem your Elohim</b> — this too is an explicit commandment, for the Torah said concerning the priests (Leviticus 21:5): \"They shall not make a bald patch on their heads and they shall not incise a cut in their flesh.\" Now He explains that it was not by virtue of the priests' superiority alone that He mentioned there that they are holy to their Elohim and commanded them this, but rather the entire congregation, all of them, are holy, and all of you are children to HaShem your Elohim like the priests. If so, guard yourselves too in this commandment just as they do. And according to the opinion of our Sages, the second prohibition applies only to the dead. And it is possible that the first commandment concerning the priests meant that if a priest was bald-patched or gashed he would be unfit for service, as it says (v. 6): \"and they shall not profane the name of their Elohim,\" and behold their service is profaned — whereas here He explains that the commandment applies also to Israel, and both were required. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Deuteronomy 14:1): \"Since you are children of the Place, you are worthy to be handsome and not gashed and bald-patched.\" This is not correct, for if so the commandment would apply even not upon the dead. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 14:1): After you know that you are children to HaShem and He loves you more than a father loves his son, do not gash yourselves over whatever He may do, for everything He does is for the good even if you do not understand it — just as small children do not understand their father's deeds but rely upon him — because you are a holy people, and you are not like all the other nations; therefore you shall not do as their deeds. In my opinion, the reason of <b>a holy people</b> is an assurance regarding the persistence of souls before Him, blessed be He — He says: since you are a holy people and the treasured possession of HaShem, and the El does not take a soul and devise plans to drive away one driven from Him, it is not fitting for you to gash and make bald patches over a soul, even if it dies in youth. And the verse does not forbid weeping, for nature is stirred to weep at the separation of loved ones and their departure even during their lifetime. And from here is support for our Sages (Mo'ed Katan 27) in their forbidding mourning over a soul more than is proper."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:20.595604864Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}